<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George II Rákóczi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1621">1621</a>–<a href="/years/1660">1660</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George II Rákóczi was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-transylvania">Prince of Transylvania</a> from 1648. Seeking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a>, he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1657 in alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> without Ottoman permission; his army was destroyed, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a> ravaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> in reprisal. Deposed by the sultan, he fought on to regain power and died of wounds received in battle in 1660.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-ii-rakoczi.jpg" alt="George II Rákóczi" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:I.r%C3%A1k%C3%B3czi_gy%C3%B6rgy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>George II Rákóczi was born on 30 January 1621, the eldest son of George I Rákóczi and Zsuzsanna Lorántffy. He was elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-transylvania">Prince of Transylvania</a> during his father's lifetime, on 19 February 1642. On 3 February 1643 he married Sophia Báthory, a granddaughter of Stephen Báthory IX, and their son was Francis I Rákóczi. On taking the throne in October 1648 his first thought was to realise his father's ambitions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In early 1649 he allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> hetman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohdan-khmelnytsky">Bohdan Khmelnytsky</a> and with the hospodars of Moldavia and Wallachia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasile-lupu">Vasile Lupu</a> and Matei Basarab, but took no action for several years. By the Treaty of Radnot of 6 December 1656 he allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> against John II Casimir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and was to seize <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a> and Mazovia with the salt deposits at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wieliczka">Wieliczka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bochnia">Bochnia</a>. In late January 1657 his army of 25,000 crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathian Mountains</a> near Krosno. Its march toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> was marked by atrocities, destruction and looting. In April it joined the Swedish army, and the combined army took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lublin">Lublin</a>, besieged Brest Litovsk and in June took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a> left to fight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, and Rákóczi withdrew. Polish forces under Stefan Czarniecki pursued him, and he was defeated at Czarny Ostrów on 20 July. At Miedzyboz on 23 July he capitulated to Jerzy Lubomirski, promising to break his alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and pay over four million złotys. On 26 July Tatars of the Crimean Khanate, then Polish allies, attacked him. He abandoned his army to János Kemény, and on 31 July the camp near Trembowla was captured, with 500 Transylvanians killed and about 11,000 taken to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 3 November 1657 the Diet deposed him at the command of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, to which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> was tributary, for an unauthorised war, and replaced him with Francis Rhédey. A new Diet at Medgyes reinstated him in January 1658. He was deposed again by the Turkish Grand Vizier and again reinstated. The Turks then invaded Transylvania, and Rákóczi died at Nagyvárad of wounds received at the battle of Gyalu in May 1660.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “George II Rákóczi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>George II Rákóczi's</b> disastrous invasion of Poland in 1657 brought Ottoman reprisals.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-transylvania">Principality of Transylvania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sárospatak, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. Died in Oradea, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.32,21.57&amp;year=1646" aria-label="Sárospatak on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5051 474 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of George II Rákóczi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5501 744h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5501 744h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.32,21.57&amp;year=1646">What was here in 1646? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
